<main class="lux-article-page"><nav class="lux-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en', '')); ?>">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Knowledge Blog</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">The Neapolitan Pizzaiuolo: Why Making Pizza Can Be Cultural Heritage</span></nav><article class="lux-reader-layout lux-academy-reader">
    <div class="lux-reader-rule"></div>
    <section class="lux-reader-hero"><div class="lux-reader-hero-copy"><div class="lux-reader-meta-grid"><span>Producers, Masters &amp; Stories of Place</span><span>Producers, Masters &amp; Stories of Place · 2026</span></div><h1 id="lux-reader-title">The Neapolitan Pizzaiuolo: Why Making Pizza Can Be Cultural Heritage</h1><p class="lux-reader-summary">Pizza has become one of the world&#39;s most common foods. In Naples, however, a pizzaiuolo is more than someone whose job is to place dough in an oven. In 2017, the Art of the Neapolitan Pizzaiuolo was inscribed on UNESCO&#39;s Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.</p></div><figure class="lux-reader-cover"><img width="2000" height="1333" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="<?php echo esc_url(get_template_directory_uri() . '/assets/media/academy/producers-neapolitan-pizzaiuolo.webp'); ?>" alt="The Neapolitan Pizzaiuolo: Why Making Pizza Can Be Cultural Heritage"></figure></section>
    <section class="lux-reader-content"><aside class="lux-reader-aside"><a href="<?php echo esc_url(luxureat_static_url('en/blog', '')); ?>">Back to Knowledge Blog</a></aside><div class="lux-reader-copy"><section class="lux-reader-section lux-reader-section-opening"><p>Importantly, the recognition concerns not pizza as an object, but the knowledge, gestures, and social practices of the people who make it.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-0"><h2>Dough Changes Through the Hands</h2><p>One of the defining features of Neapolitan pizza is the contrast between a soft centre and a raised, aerated rim.</p><p>Achieving this structure requires more than knowing ingredient weights.</p><p>The pizzaiuolo must judge:</p><p>fermentation;</p><p>dough elasticity;</p><p>pressure during shaping;</p><p>how to preserve gas in the rim;</p><p>and how to extend the dough efficiently.</p><p>UNESCO documentation even records characteristic manual gestures used to stretch the dough and form the cornicione.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-1"><h2>The Oven Leaves Little Time for Correction</h2><p>Traditional Neapolitan pizza is baked at very high temperatures.</p><p>This leaves little time for hesitation.</p><p>Dough thickness, topping moisture, oven position, and rotation all require rapid judgement.</p><p>A skilled pizzaiuolo is therefore not simply repeating a recipe.</p><p>The craft consists of making decisions continuously.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-2"><h2>What the Master Passes On Is Movement</h2><p>Pizzaiuolo skills have traditionally been transmitted through practice.</p><p>Apprentices observe:</p><p>how the dough feels;</p><p>how it is stretched;</p><p>how ingredients are distributed;</p><p>how the oven is used;</p><p>how the finished pizza is judged.</p><p>This is why the tradition is considered living heritage.</p><p>A tradition survives not because instructions remain in a book.</p><p>It survives because people continue to perform it.</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-3"><h2>Why Naples Cannot Be Removed From the Story</h2><p>Excellent Neapolitan-style pizza can now be made in many countries.</p><p>But the reason this style developed cannot be separated from Naples, its urban history, street-food culture, workshops, and social life.</p><p>A story of origin does not need to claim:</p><p>“Only Naples can make pizza.”</p><p>It explains:</p><p>“Why did this particular pizza culture develop in Naples?”</p></section><section class="lux-reader-section" id="article-section-4"><h2>LuxurEat(露意膳) Perspective</h2><p>When LuxurEat(露意膳) presents pizza flour, tomatoes, extra virgin olive oil, or related ingredients, the most useful approach is not to put “authentic Italian” on everything.</p><p>It is to explain:</p><p>which pizza style the product supports;</p><p>what texture that style is trying to achieve;</p><p>why flour, hydration, fermentation, and oven conditions matter.</p><p>Once consumers understand the work of the pizzaiuolo, the ingredients themselves become easier to understand.</p></section></div></section>
  </article></main>
